# Writing a SMAPpy plugin in this folder

This folder holds the user's own SMAPpy plugins.  SMAPpy is a Python program
for single-molecule localization microscopy (SMLM); every analysis in it is a
plugin, and a `.py` file saved here is one as soon as the GUI's Plugins menu is
opened again -- no registration, no restart, no install.

You (an AI assistant, or a person) are here to turn what the user describes --
"the distance of each localization to the nearest one in the other channel",
"how many localizations per cluster, as a histogram" -- into such a file.

## What is asked of a plugin here, and what is not

These are the user's plugins, so the rules are few:

* **Required**: the shape below, and `changes_locs` (the program refuses a
  plugin class without it).
* **Recommended, not required**: a help page (`<file>.md` beside the
  plugin), tests, a `version`.  Offer them; do not insist.  A use-once plugin
  for one dataset needs neither.
* A plugin that is to be **shipped with SMAPpy** (a pull request to
  `ries-lab/SMAPpy`) has to follow the repository's rules -- its `CLAUDE.md`:
  a page, tests on simulated data, the house style.  See "Sharing it" below.

## Where files go

* `thing.py` here -> the plugin tree's **User/Thing** (the file name, title
  cased; `name = "..."` on the class overrides it).
* `Analysis/Measure/thing.py` (any subfolders) -> **Analysis/Measure/Thing**,
  beside the shipped plugins.  The folders are the tree.
* One plugin class per file.  A file whose name starts with `_` is skipped
  (`_template.py` is the example to copy).
* A plugin at the same path as a shipped one replaces it -- on purpose, or
  rename yours.
* The plugin is opened from the GUI's **Plugins** menu (User > Thing) or
  pinned to a tab with a tab's **+** button.

## The shape

```python
from dataclasses import dataclass
import numpy as np
from smappy.plugins import Context, Plugin, Result, param

def my_measure(x, y):                     # the algorithm: arrays in, numbers out
    ...

@dataclass
class ThingSettings:                       # the form the GUI builds
    radius_nm: float = param(50.0, label="radius", unit="nm", min=0.1,
                             help="the tooltip")

class Thing(Plugin):
    """One line: what it does (the GUI shows it)."""
    Settings = ThingSettings
    changes_locs = False                   # True if run hands back a new table

    def run(self, ctx: Context, settings: ThingSettings) -> Result:
        locs = ctx.selection.apply(ctx.locs)   # what the user is looking at
        value = my_measure(locs["x_nm"], locs["y_nm"])
        return Result(text=f"...", plot=lambda ax: ax.hist(...),
                      settings=settings)
```

`_template.py` in this folder is a complete, working example.  To start a new
plugin from it: `smappy-plugins new "my thing"` (or Plugins > New plugin...
in the GUI), which writes `my_thing.py` with the class renamed.

Imports at the top of the file are fine for numpy and smappy; put heavy ones
(scipy, sklearn, torch) inside the function that uses them, so the program
starts fast.  Do not import Qt: the GUI builds the form from the settings.

## The settings: `param(default, ...)`

A dataclass field; the GUI makes a widget of it, by its type: `float`/`int`
a number box, `bool` a tick, `str` with `choices=` a menu, `str` alone a
line of text, `Optional[float] = param(None, ...)` a box that may be empty.

| keyword | |
| --- | --- |
| `label` | what the form shows (short: the form is narrow) |
| `unit` | shown beside the box: `"nm"`, `"frames"` |
| `min`, `max` | bounds |
| `help` | the tooltip, a short phrase |
| `choices` | `("a", "b")` or `(("value", "label"), ...)` |
| `advanced=True` | hidden under *more* |
| `kind` | `"open_file"`, `"save_file"`, `"dir"` (a path with a browse button), `"text"` (a wide line) |

A field whose type is another settings dataclass is a section of its own.

## What `run` gets: the context

* `ctx.locs` -- the table the current layer shows (one row per blink if the
  layer merges blinks).  `ctx.selection` -- what the user is looking at
  (the layer's filter, the ROI) as a boolean mask over it;
  `ctx.selection.apply(ctx.locs)` is that subset.  Use it unless the plugin
  is meant to see everything.
* `ctx.table(i)` -- layer `i`'s `(locs, selection)`: for a plugin that
  compares channels or layers.  `ctx.session.layers` lists them (`.name`,
  `.visible`, `.is_image`); `ctx.session` is None in a script.
* `ctx.report("text")` -- progress, shown in the panel;
  `ctx.warn("text")` -- a warning that must not be missed.
* `ctx.stopped` -- True when the user pressed Stop; check it in long loops.
* `ctx.region` -- in a per-region run, the ROI's shape; `ctx.per_region`
  says whether this is one.

## The table: `Localizations`

`locs["x_nm"]` is a numpy array; `"x_nm" in locs`; `locs.keys()`;
`len(locs)`; `locs[mask]` a new table of those rows.  Never change `ctx.locs`
in place.

Columns are `<quantity>_<unit>`, uncertainties `<quantity>_err_<unit>`.  The
common ones: `x_nm`, `y_nm`, `z_nm` (3D only), `frame`, `photons`,
`background`, `xy_err_nm`, `z_err_nm`, `sigma_nm`, `logl_rel`, `channel`;
after merging blinks `group_id` and `n_in_group`; after other plugins
`cluster_id`, `n_in_cluster`, `track_id`.  Not every table has every column:
check, and when one is missing raise a `ValueError` that names the columns
the table does have.  A `ValueError` is shown to the user as the reason the
run failed.

## What `run` hands back: `Result`

Every part is optional.

| field | |
| --- | --- |
| `text` | a summary, shown in the panel |
| `plot` | `draw(ax)`, a function drawing on one matplotlib axis -- make it a closure over the data |
| `plots` | more figures, `{"name": draw}` |
| `data` | any numbers or arrays, for a script |
| `values` | `{"name": number}`: with `per_region = True`, one row per ROI in the region table |
| `locs` | a **new** table that replaces the current one (then `changes_locs = True`) |
| `settings` | the settings used -- always pass them |

For several axes in one figure: `from smappy.plugins import Plot` and
`plot=Plot(draw, panels=2)`, where `draw(fig)` makes them with
`fig.subplots(1, 2)`.  Never set a figure's size or layout.

A plugin that **adds a column**: copy the table and add it --

```python
new = Localizations(dict(locs.columns), dict(locs.metadata))   # from smappy.locs
new.columns["my_value"] = values                                 # one per row of ctx.locs
return Result(locs=new, settings=settings)
```

-- with `changes_locs = True` and `grouping = "ungrouped"` on the class (a new
table is made from the localizations, not the merged blinks).

## Class attributes worth knowing

* `name = "Thing distance"` -- what the GUI shows, if not the file name.
* `per_region = True` -- it hands back `Result.values`, so it can run once
  per ROI or cluster.
* `grouping = "ungrouped"` -- always the localizations, never merged blinks.
* `follow_roi = True` -- for work taking tens of milliseconds: a tick that
  re-runs it while the ROI is dragged.
* `version = "2"` -- bump it when a change moves the numbers.

## Checking it

Without the GUI, on simulated data:

```python
from smappy.simulate import simulate
from smappy.plugins.discovery import load_module
module = load_module("thing.py")             # this folder's file
locs = simulate(seed=1)                      # a simulated table
result = module.Thing()(locs)                # runs it on all of it
print(result.text)                           # Thing()(locs, radius_nm=30): other settings
```

`smappy-plugins check` (or `python -m smappy.user_plugins check`) imports
every plugin in this folder and lists what fails: a syntax error, two plugin
classes in one file, a missing `changes_locs`.

## More to read

* The shipped plugins are good examples, in `@@PACKAGE@@/plugins/`:
  `histogram.py` (a measurement per layer), `statistics.py` (several
  figures), `math_parser.py` (a new column), `remove_locs.py` (a filter).
* The full reference -- every field of the context and the result, the
  column names: `docs/plugin-reference.md` in the repository,
  https://github.com/ries-lab/SMAPpy/blob/main/docs/plugin-reference.md
* For a person: the help page *Your own plugins* (Help > Plugin
  documentation).

## Sharing it

A plugin that others would use can be shipped with SMAPpy: open a pull
request to https://github.com/ries-lab/SMAPpy that adds it under
`src/smappy/plugins/` with `@register("Tab/Group/Name")`.  There it has to
follow the repository's `CLAUDE.md`: a help page in
`src/smappy/plugins/docs/`, tests on simulated data that check the numbers,
and the house style.  Those rules are for what is shipped; nothing here needs
them.
